14. OFFLINE-FIRST ARCHITECTURE AND NETWORK SYNCHRONIZATION

This is one of the most important requirements of the application.

I want the application to work smoothly in both online and offline scenarios.

Review the entire existing offline-first architecture.

14.1 Online Behavior

When the internet is available:

- Fetch fresh data from the remote data source.
- Persist successful responses in Room.
- Observe database changes through Flow.
- Update the UI automatically.
- Synchronize local cart and other eligible pending operations.
- Handle server responses correctly.
- Avoid displaying stale data when fresh data is available.

14.2 Offline Behavior

When the internet is unavailable:

- Allow users to browse previously cached restaurants.
- Allow users to browse cached categories and menus.
- Allow users to add and remove cart items.
- Persist cart changes locally.
- Allow users to review the checkout information.
- Handle order placement according to the defined offline-ordering policy.

IMPORTANT ORDER PLACEMENT POLICY:

An order must not be falsely presented as confirmed when the server has not accepted it.

If a user attempts to place an order while offline:

- Persist the order intent locally if the existing business rules permit it.
- Mark it internally as pending submission.
- Do not generate a confirmed order ID or confirmed status without server acknowledgement.
- Display a clear network error or an appropriate offline-order message to the user.
- Retry submission when connectivity returns, according to a safe retry policy.
- Prevent duplicate orders using idempotency keys or an equivalent mechanism.

When the server successfully accepts the order, update the local order state.

